course: dictionary
lesson: 5641
title: What is linguistics?
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Linguistics is the scientific study of language. A linguist asks how sounds, words and grammar work, how languages change over time, and how people learn them. «In a UKLO Breakthrough puzzle, you meet a language you have never seen and work out its rules from a few examples.» That is linguistics in action: you study a language the way a detective studies clues.
explain: UKLO means the UK Linguistics Olympiad. Breakthrough is its first, easiest level, so it is a good place to start. You do not need to speak the language in the puzzle. You only need careful looking and sensible thinking.
explain: Linguists often study patterns in languages they do not speak, because the patterns are what matter. Linguistics is also a university subject, so people can study it for years and make it their career.
fact: Linguistics is the scientific study of language.
fact: Linguistics looks at sounds, words, grammar and meaning.
fact: Linguists also study how languages change over time and how people learn them.
fact: «Linguistics» is taught as a subject at university.
fact: The UK Linguistics Olympiad (UKLO) is a puzzle contest, and «Breakthrough» is its first, easiest level.
exercise: What is linguistics? | *The scientific study of language | A kind of maths puzzle | The study of stars and planets | Learning to draw and paint
exercise: «In a UKLO Breakthrough puzzle, you meet a language you have never seen.» What is the best way to solve it? | *Work out its rules from a few examples, like a detective | Ask someone to translate every single word | Guess one word at random | Skip the puzzle, because you cannot speak the language
exercise: In the UK Linguistics Olympiad, what does «Breakthrough» mean? | A special prize | *The first, easiest level | The hardest level | The final round
test: Which parts of language does linguistics study? | Only the names of countries | Only numbers and shapes | *Sounds, words, grammar and meaning | Only spelling
test: Do linguists need to speak every language they study? | Only if the language is easy | Yes, but only at university | Yes, always, before they can start | *No, they can study the patterns in languages they do not speak
exam: Why do linguists study languages they may not speak? | To learn to speak them perfectly | *To find the patterns and rules hidden in them | Because those languages are always the hardest | To win a university prize
